[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — one shared logger, two runtimes. Next fork, and it's the big one.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether browser logs stay in the user's console (where you can never see them) or get shipped to a collector
  you can query. Everything else — buffering, batching, an endpoint, failure handling when the network is down —
  follows from this and is expensive to retrofit.

  - [1mConsole only:[0m buys you a clean, zero-infrastructure logger today; costs you the actual goal, since "debug production
    issues" means reading logs from users' machines you don't have access to. Cheap to add shipping later [3mif[0m the logger
    has a pluggable output seam from the start.
  - [1mShip to an endpoint:[0m buys you real production visibility; costs an ingest endpoint (yours or a vendor's), a
    batching/retry path, and a decision about what's safe to transmit. Hard to bolt on cleanly later, because it changes
    the logger from synchronous to buffered.
  - [1mConsole now, seam for shipping:[0m buys you a working logger this week with the transport boundary designed in but
    unimplemented; costs a little indirection you don't use yet. This is the honest middle.

  I recommend the third unless you already have somewhere to send logs. The stub [38;5;153mlogin()[39m doesn't call a real API yet, so
  there may not be a backend to receive logs either — a transport designed against a server that doesn't exist yet
  tends to get the interface wrong.
