[1m[38;5;231mWhere should browser logs end up in production?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mConsole now, seam later (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mLogger writes to console but routes through a pluggable sink interface, so adding a remote transport later is one [39m
     [38;5;246mnew sink, not a rewrite.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Ship to our own endpoint
     [38;5;246mBatch and POST logs to a backend route we define. Needs a real endpoint, retry/offline handling, and a payload size
[39m     [38;5;246mbudget.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m Ship to a vendor
     [38;5;246mSentry / Datadog / similar. Least code, most capability, but adds a third-party dependency and sends user data to [39m
     [38;5;246mthem.[39m
  [38;5;246m4.[39m Console only, no seam
     [38;5;246mSimplest possible: structured console output, nothing remote, no indirection. Accepts that production browser logs
[39m     [38;5;246mstay unreachable.[39m
  [38;5;246m5. Type something.[39m
